1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,280
The Frontier model DeepSeek V4 Pro has

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:09,440
1.6 trillion parameters stored in FP8 1

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:12,480
byte per parameter that comes to about

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:15,200
1.6 terabytes of weights.

5
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:18,600
The memory on a high-end Nvidia B200 is

6
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:21,080
192 GB. So, the weights alone are eight

7
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:24,840
times too big for one chip, 20 times too

8
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:26,920
big for an 80 GB H100.

9
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:29,200
And this is not a gap quantization can

10
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:31,520
close. Even if you could shrink every

11
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,560
parameter to a single bit, the weights

12
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:36,360
would still overflow the B200.

13
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:38,840
The model has to be split across many

14
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:40,920
GPUs, and the moment you split it, a

15
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:42,600
problem appears that a single chip never

16
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:44,760
had.

17
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:47,800
On a single GPU, there is nothing to

18
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:50,280
coordinate. Weights, activations, and

19
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:52,680
the KV cache, the keys and values for

20
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:55,240
every prior token, all live in one

21
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,760
memory, and each layer's output feeds

22
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:59,840
the next layer directly.

23
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:03,160
Now, put the first half of the layers on

24
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:05,680
GPU A and the second half on B.

25
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:08,040
B cannot start until A's output crosses

26
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:11,080
a wire, and while that transfer is in

27
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:13,400
flight, B sits [music] idle. Time spent

28
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:15,360
communicating is time not spent

29
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:18,200
computing.

30
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:21,200
Splitting turns a compute problem into a

31
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,280
compute plus communication problem.

32
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:24,880
For the rest of the video, we will put

33
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:26,320
the same two questions to every

34
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,760
strategy.

35
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:31,700
What does it have to send and how often?

36
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,680
Two memory pressures force the split.

37
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,840
[music]

38
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:37,560
The first is the weights, already too

39
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:40,200
large for any single chip.

40
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:42,840
The second builds during generation.

41
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:45,080
Each new token attends over every token

42
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:47,880
before it, and that comparison needs a

43
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:50,680
key vector and a value vector for every

44
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:52,800
earlier position at every layer.

45
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:54,800
Recomputing those at every step would

46
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,840
repeat nearly all the work of the steps

47
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:01,640
before, [music] so the model computes

48
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:03,680
them once and keeps them, the KV cache.

49
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:06,479
The cache grows with every token of

50
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:08,880
context and every request in the batch

51
00:02:06,479 --> 00:02:10,080
and multiplies again across every layer

52
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:12,200
of the model.

53
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:15,360
For a 70 billion parameter model with a

54
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:18,640
16-bit cache, a 128,000

55
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:21,160
token context already costs around 40 GB

56
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:23,680
for a single request.

57
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:25,840
At long context, the cache can reach the

58
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:28,200
size of the weights themselves [music]

59
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:30,720
and a million token sequence overflows a

60
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:32,880
GPU on its own.

61
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:34,440
Split the model and the GPUs must

62
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:36,120
exchange data.

63
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:39,040
The exchanges come in a handful of

64
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:41,160
standard patterns called collectives

65
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:44,280
and each strategy in this video will

66
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:46,800
have one as its signature.

67
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:49,720
The workhorse is the all reduce.

68
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:52,880
Every GPU contributes a partial result

69
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:54,760
and every GPU gets back the total.

70
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:55,959
Its two halves also show up on their

71
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:59,400
own.

72
00:02:55,959 --> 00:03:02,720
An all gather collects every GPU's piece

73
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:05,120
onto every GPU without summing,

74
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:07,720
while a reduce scatter does the sum but

75
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:09,680
leaves each GPU holding just one slice

76
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:12,120
of the total.

77
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:14,480
The heaviest pattern is the all to all

78
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:17,400
where every GPU sends a different chunk

79
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:19,280
to every other GPU at once.

80
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:22,239
And the lightest is barely a collective

81
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:24,120
at all, a point-to-point send, one GPU

82
00:03:22,239 --> 00:03:26,519
to one other.

83
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:28,680
Which pattern a strategy fires and how

84
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:30,640
often decides where on the hardware it

85
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:32,440
belongs.

86
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:34,400
The hardware these patterns run on is

87
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:37,440
not uniform.

88
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:39,440
Inside one server, a node, GPUs,

89
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:42,320
typically eight of them, are wired

90
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:44,959
directly to each other over NVLink,

91
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:48,680
Nvidia's dedicated GPU to GPU

92
00:03:44,959 --> 00:03:50,239
interconnect, 900 GB per second on an

93
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,760
H100,

94
00:03:50,239 --> 00:03:55,800
1.8

95
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:58,600
TB on a B200.

96
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:00,880
Step outside the box and traffic leaves

97
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:03,720
through the data center network instead,

98
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:08,080
InfiniBand or Ethernet at about 50 GB

99
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:10,600
per second, 18 to 36 times slower.

100
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:14,120
Nvidia's newest rack-scale systems

101
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:16,600
stretch a single NVLink domain across 72

102
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:19,600
GPUs, but the shape of the world is the

103
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:21,720
same. Wherever the NVLink domain ends,

104
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:25,120
the drop is waiting.

105
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:28,200
That single gap governs the layout.

106
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:31,400
Heavy, constant traffic must stay inside

107
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:33,680
the fast NVLink domain. Only thin,

108
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:36,720
occasional traffic can afford to cross

109
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:38,240
the slow boundary between boxes.

110
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:39,919
Megatron-LM,

111
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:42,680
Nvidia's model parallel training

112
00:04:39,919 --> 00:04:44,480
framework, defined the standard recipe,

113
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:47,440
tensor parallelism.

114
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:50,240
Split each weight matrix across GPUs.

115
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:53,400
So, one matrix multiply becomes several

116
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:56,040
partial ones running at once.

117
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:58,800
A feed-forward block expands [music] its

118
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:00,320
input through matrix A, applies a

119
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:02,520
non-linearity,

120
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:05,040
GELU, [music]

121
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:07,560
then contracts through matrix B.

122
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:09,760
Megatron splits A by columns, and

123
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:12,480
because GELU touches each number

124
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:15,440
independently, every GPU computes its

125
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:18,160
slice with no communication at all.

126
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:20,400
B is split by rows to match, which

127
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:23,160
leaves [music] each GPU an output of the

128
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:25,440
right shape, but only part of the value.

129
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:27,360
One all reduce sums the partials into

130
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:30,200
the true result.

131
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:32,720
Attention divides even more naturally,

132
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:35,600
since its heads are already independent.

133
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:37,560
Each GPU runs a share of the heads, and

134
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,760
one more all reduce recombines [music]

135
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:41,000
them.

136
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:43,640
So, the price is two all reduces per

137
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,919
layer on the critical path. The next

138
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:48,160
layer cannot start until they they

139
00:05:45,919 --> 00:05:51,400
For the 80-layer model from earlier,

140
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:53,960
that is 160 blocking exchanges per

141
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:55,160
token, far too heavy to cross between

142
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:56,960
servers.

143
00:05:55,160 --> 00:06:00,240
The Megatron team therefore capped the

144
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:02,874
group at the GPUs inside one server, the

145
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:03,000
eight NVLink GPUs of a standard node,

146
00:06:02,874 --> 00:06:05,520
>> [music]

147
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,800
>> and the rule still holds.

148
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:09,840
The reward is that each GPU holds

149
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:11,760
roughly an eighth of the weights and

150
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:13,680
does an eighth of the math.

151
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:15,960
This is the strategy that makes a single

152
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:17,960
token faster.

153
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:20,919
Pipeline parallelism splits the model

154
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:23,440
the obvious way, by layers.

155
00:06:20,919 --> 00:06:25,840
Each GPU group takes one contiguous

156
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:27,440
block, like stations on an assembly

157
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:29,840
line.

158
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:31,800
A batch flows through stage one,

159
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:35,480
which hands its output activations to

160
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:37,360
stage two, and on down the line.

161
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:38,400
Now compare the traffic to what we just

162
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:40,520
saw.

163
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:43,720
Tensor parallelism synchronized every

164
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:46,520
GPU twice inside every layer.

165
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:49,560
A pipeline stage speaks once,

166
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:52,600
at its own boundary, to one neighbor.

167
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:55,000
A single point-to-point message.

168
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:57,080
Traffic that thin is easy to hide. The

169
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,204
stage sends one batch's activations

170
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:01,480
onward while it computes the next,

171
00:06:59,204 --> 00:07:03,640
[music] so the transfer adds almost no

172
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:05,960
wall clock time.

173
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:08,760
That makes pipeline parallelism the

174
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:11,120
natural way to span nodes. Thin, [music]

175
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:15,160
occasional traffic is exactly what can

176
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:18,000
afford the slow boundary between boxes.

177
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:19,520
Spanning nodes cheaply has a cost.

178
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,520
While stage one works on the first

179
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:24,095
batch, every stage after it sits idle

180
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:24,480
waiting for work to arrive.

181
00:07:24,095 --> 00:07:26,000
>> [music]

182
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:28,040
>> And by the time the batch reaches the

183
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,880
last stage, the early stages have

184
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:32,080
already run dry.

185
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:35,440
Fill at the start, drain at the end.

186
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:38,000
That idle time is the pipeline bubble.

187
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:40,120
The fix is microbatches. Break the batch

188
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:42,320
into smaller slices and feed them in one

189
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:46,200
after another, so several are in flight

190
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:48,680
at once, and every stage stays busy.

191
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:50,680
The reason more micro batches help is

192
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:52,800
that filling and draining is a fixed

193
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:53,570
cost, roughly one trip through the

194
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:53,720
stages,

195
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:55,880
>> [music]

196
00:07:53,720 --> 00:07:57,520
>> while the useful work grows with every

197
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:59,920
extra slice.

198
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:03,640
Four stages fed four micro batches idle

199
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:06,200
near 43% feed them 32 and the bubble

200
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:08,200
drops to 9%.

201
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:09,880
And one more trick comes from the

202
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:12,080
training world.

203
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:14,480
Give each GPU several short

204
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:17,200
non-contiguous chunks of layers instead

205
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:19,840
of one block, so the pipeline fills and

206
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:22,960
drains in smaller steps at the price of

207
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:24,800
more messages between stages.

208
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:27,360
The bubble is not the same size for

209
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:29,560
every job because serving has two very

210
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:32,120
different phases.

211
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:34,360
Prefill reads the whole prompt at once,

212
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:36,960
maybe thousands of tokens in one compute

213
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:39,680
heavy pass, plenty of work to slice into

214
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:41,960
micro batches, so the pipe fills and the

215
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:44,960
bubble stays small.

216
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:47,360
Decode is the opposite. Each new token

217
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:49,600
depends on the one before it, so a

218
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:50,440
single request can only move through the

219
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:53,320
pipeline [music]

220
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:56,520
one tiny step at a time, and the stages

221
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:59,200
spend most of their time waiting.

222
00:08:56,520 --> 00:09:01,120
The fix is to fill the pipe with other

223
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:03,000
people's tokens.

224
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:05,400
Continuous batching folds new requests

225
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,120
in as old ones finish, keeping every

226
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:09,280
stage fed.

227
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:11,280
And modern stacks go further and run

228
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:14,040
prefill and decode on separate GPU

229
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:16,080
pools, each with its own parallelism, so

230
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:18,800
the heavy prompt work never stalls the

231
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:21,040
token-by-token stream.

232
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:23,520
A mixture of experts layer takes the

233
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:25,920
feed forward block, the biggest block of

234
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:28,560
weights in the model, and breaks it into

235
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:31,920
many smaller independent ones called

236
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:33,040
experts, and each token uses only a few

237
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:36,920
of them.

238
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:39,400
In DeepSeek V4 Pro, a layer holds 384

239
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:41,680
routed experts, and every token runs

240
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:43,840
through just six of them.

241
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:46,360
Why models built this way work so well

242
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:48,040
is the next video's topic. What matters

243
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:50,120
here is that the experts are separate

244
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:54,360
networks, so they can live on separate

245
00:09:50,120 --> 00:09:55,920
GPUs. That split is expert parallelism.

246
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:56,960
Now, watch one token move through a

247
00:09:55,920 --> 00:09:59,240
layer.

248
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:01,120
It arrives on some GPU and a small

249
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:02,480
router reads it and picks its six

250
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:04,320
experts.

251
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:06,520
A couple might live on this chip, but

252
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:08,560
the rest sit elsewhere. So, the token

253
00:10:06,520 --> 00:10:10,960
ships out to the GPUs that hold its

254
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:13,840
experts, gets processed there, and the

255
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:15,640
results come back to be combined.

256
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:17,760
Multiply that by every token in the

257
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:20,240
batch, each with its own list of

258
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:23,080
experts, and the picture becomes every

259
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:24,720
GPU sending tokens to every other GPU at

260
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:27,280
once.

261
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:30,720
That is the all-to-all twice per MoE

262
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:32,520
layer, a dispatch out, a combined back.

263
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:34,760
And because the router redraws the map

264
00:10:32,520 --> 00:10:37,160
for every batch, the traffic cannot be

265
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:39,680
planned in advance.

266
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:42,839
What surprised me in the measurements is

267
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:46,240
how completely the shuffling takes over.

268
00:10:42,839 --> 00:10:50,280
Across nodes, those two all-to-alls can

269
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:53,000
eat over 70% of a MoE layer's time and

270
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:54,320
around 40% of end-to-end inference

271
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,680
latency.

272
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:59,720
The expert math is not the bottleneck.

273
00:10:56,680 --> 00:11:02,520
Moving tokens to the experts is.

274
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:04,920
That is why DeepSeek built DPP, an

275
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:07,760
all-to-all library that exists just for

276
00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:09,880
dispatch and combine.

277
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:12,040
Data parallelism is the simplest

278
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:14,440
strategy of the five.

279
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:17,200
Run complete independent copies of the

